\section{Software Process Models}

\subsection*{Motivation}
Choosing a process model is like choosing a route to travel from your home to a new city—some routes are straight highways (waterfall), others are stop‑and‑go buses (iterative).  Different routes help teams reach the destination efficiently under different conditions.  Understanding the strengths and weaknesses of each model lets you match a project’s uncertainty, risk, and schedule to the most suitable path.  This section explains the major models and how they relate to effort and defect distribution.

\subsection*{Intuition}
A software process is simply the ordered set of activities a team follows to turn ideas into a working program.  Some teams move linearly from requirements to deployment, while others loop back, build quick sketches, or fix a time limit for each cycle.  The key is to pick the pattern that reduces risk, handles changing requirements, and fits the amount of time and resources available.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
A **software process** is a collection of steps that produce software.  The **distribution of effort** across the life‑cycle typically allocates roughly 10–20 % of total effort to Requirements, 10–20 % to Design, 20–30 % to Coding, and 30–50 % to Testing.  Although **Coding** exhibits the highest **defect density** (over 50 % of defects originate there), it is not the most expensive phase because testing consumes the largest share of effort and resources.

The four major process models are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Waterfall}: A strictly linear sequence of phases – Requirements $\rightarrow$ High‑Level Design $\rightarrow$ Detailed Design $\rightarrow$ Coding $\rightarrow$ Testing $\rightarrow$ Deployment.  Each phase finishes before the next begins, producing extensive documentation.  It works well when the problem is well understood and requirements are unlikely to change.  A common mistake is to think the waterfall model can easily accommodate frequent requirement changes; in reality, late changes are costly.
  \item \textbf{Prototyping}: The team quickly builds a **prototype**—a simplified, often “quick and dirty” version of the system—to explore and clarify requirements.  Feedback from users refines the requirements before full development starts.  It is important not to assume the prototype becomes the final product; typically the prototype is discarded or heavily reworked.
  \item \textbf{Iterative Development}: The overall project is divided into a series of mini‑waterfalls, each delivering a functional increment.  Lessons from one iteration feed into the next, avoiding unnecessary features and allowing gradual improvement.  While this reduces feature bloat, an unplanned architecture can suffer if the overall structure is not considered early.
  \item \textbf{Timeboxing}: A fixed length of time (the “box”) is assigned to an iteration, and the team selects the subset of features that fit inside that box.  If the scope is too large, some features are postponed.  The schedule is therefore predictable, and stages such as design, coding, and testing can be pipelined to run in parallel.  Timeboxing does not mean sacrificing quality; quality must be built into the work that fits within the box.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Problem statement:} A small team is commissioned to create a mobile‑app that displays local restaurant menus.  The client is unsure about the exact layout and interaction style for the user interface (UI).  Determine which process model should be used to gather requirements, and then decide whether iterative development or timeboxing is more appropriate for the later development phases.

\textbf{Step 1 – Identify the main uncertainty.}  
The uncertainty lies in the UI design; the functional back‑end (fetching menus) is well understood.

\textbf{Step 2 – Choose a model to reduce this uncertainty.}  
Because the goal is to explore and clarify UI requirements, the **prototyping** model is ideal.  A quick mock‑up of the app screens can be shown to users, whose feedback will shape the final UI specifications.

\textbf{Step 3 – Evaluate the next phase options.}  
After the UI requirements are solidified, the project still faces two constraints: (a) the client wants a usable product within six weeks, and (b) the team wants to deliver value early.

\textbf{Step 4 – Compare iterative development and timeboxing.}  
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Iterative development} would break the remaining work into several increments (e.g., basic menu display, search feature, user reviews).  Each increment would be a mini‑waterfall, allowing the team to incorporate feedback from earlier increments.
  \item \textbf{Timeboxing} would fix the six‑week schedule and select the most critical features that fit within that window (e.g., core menu display and simple search).  Less critical features would be postponed to a later release.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 5 – Decide the best fit.}  
Because the overall schedule is tight (six weeks) and the team already has clear UI requirements, **timeboxing** is the better choice for the follow‑up phases.  It guarantees delivery of a functional core app within the deadline while allowing the team to pipeline design, coding, and testing.

\textbf{Final answer:} Use prototyping to clarify UI requirements, then adopt timeboxing for the remaining development to meet the fixed schedule.

\textbf{What this shows us:} Selecting a process model starts by matching the model’s strength to the project’s biggest uncertainty; once that uncertainty is resolved, the schedule‑driven nature of timeboxing can ensure timely delivery, whereas iterative development is preferable when the timeline is flexible and continuous feedback is desired.

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
Choose the process model whose main characteristic (linear, exploratory, incremental, or schedule‑fixed) best addresses the project’s dominant uncertainty and constraints.

